WEBVTT

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.710
The International Space Station.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.900
It's the satellite of science, paving the way for future exploration

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.880
and habitation on worlds beyond our own.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:17.870
But all that doesn't come without challenges.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.800
NASA has to work hard to keep ISS in orbit.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.160
Thanks to math, the space agency can provide a boost to keep it going.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.840
We'll show you how they do it next on Real World.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.430
Construction of the International Space Station got underway in 1998, thanks to NASA and space

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.750
agencies from several other nations of the world.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.020
Since then, they have been working to complete the project.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:59.530
Lots of work is going on right now to enable ISS to double its crew size from 3 to 6.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:10.400
And the work that's done on the station is helping scientists understand how space conditions affect people, plants, microorganisms, just about all of life.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:18.800
This work will help pave the way as we move beyond ISS to lunar habitation and human exploration of Mars and beyond.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.040
But life in space isn't always easy.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:29.980
Recently, astronauts in the space station had to hunker down in the Soyuz Escape Module, waiting to see if a chunk of space...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.510
debris would hit the station. The debris is a chunk of an upper-stage rocket used

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:42.190
to launch a GPS satellite more than 15 years ago. Fortunately, the debris passed

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:48.340
harmlessly. That was a pretty extreme situation. Usually the station has enough

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.970
advanced warning to fire its engines and adjust its orbit, thus avoiding any

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:58.620
space debris. The station also needs to adjust its orbit to compensate for gravity.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.060
Gravity is the force of attraction between all masses in the universe.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.870
Gravity is what holds us on the Earth.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.830
Richard Biles is the director of education at the Virginia Air and Space Center.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.340
Everything that has mass has gravity.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.140
The weight of an object equals mass times gravity.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:28.620
The mass of an object doesn't change, but weight does, depending on the force of gravity

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.440
on that object.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.780
Weight is directly proportional to the force of gravity on an object.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.720
That's why things weigh more on Earth than on the Moon.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.170
The Earth has more mass, thus a stronger gravitational pull.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.990
More mass equals more gravity.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.060
That's why all the planets, even the ones demoted to dwarf planets like Pluto,

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.160
way, way, way, way, way out there, are held in the gravitational field of our Sun.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.970
Each day, the station is pulled a little closer...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.340
to the Earth, losing about 90 meters of altitude.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.870
That's about the distance from goal line to goal line

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:06.990
on a football field.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.040
This is due to drag as the space station collides

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:11.880
with air molecules.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.020
Drag slows the space station down,

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.650
making it more susceptible to the force of gravity.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.900
This orbital decay is counteracted in several ways.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.210
The station's main rockets can provide a small bump.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.300
Resupply vessels that visit the ISS can push it up more.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.290
and the space shuttle has the power to provide big boosts.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.770
The orbital decay of the International Space Station

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.090
can be charted on a graph,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.750
but the rate of decay is not constant.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.480
The rate is affected by several variables.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.660
By plotting points showing the station's altitude

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.460
over its lifetime, engineers can see trends

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.180
and develop strategies for keeping the station in orbit.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.870
NASA needs to keep it above this point, 200 kilometers.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:03:59.600
Below that,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.620
Earth's atmosphere is too thick and will cause too much drag on the ISS.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.190
There is a significant dip between 2000 and 2001.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.000
This is because of a period of intense sunspots.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.060
which warmed the Earth's atmosphere.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.880
causing it to expand out to a higher altitude.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.680
creating more drag on the station.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.670
making it drop even faster.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.380
Fortunately, the Space Shuttle was able to provide a big boost

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.420
sending it back into a higher orbit.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.350
and you can see that on the graph.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.060
Sunspot activity also follows patterns.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.280
so the trends can be predicted.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:41.910
Similar sunspot activity is predicted beginning in 2012.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.430
by which time the space shuttle program is expected to be retired.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.210
Adjustments will come from the thrust of the station's two main engines.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.280
and from resupply vessels that visit ISS.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.960
NASA scientists and

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:05.000
engineers are looking into new ways to provide extra boosts that will keep the station in

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.560
orbit through the end of its mission.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:15.440
Keep track of this and all of NASA's missions at www.nasa.gov.
